1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,608
So here's a pretty normal DeFi request.

2
00:00:02,648 --> 00:00:06,915
Stake five ETH with Lido, use that as collateral on Aave,

3
00:00:06,955 --> 00:00:09,272
and borrow a thousand USDC.

4
00:00:09,312 --> 00:00:12,523
And the same thing on Solana: stake SOL with Marinade,

5
00:00:12,563 --> 00:00:17,475
post the mSOL on Kamino, and borrow a thousand USDC.

6
00:00:19,525 --> 00:00:22,411
On the left is Claude Code with no harness.

7
00:00:22,451 --> 00:00:25,905
It has to go find the contract addresses, fetch the ABIs,

8
00:00:25,945 --> 00:00:29,539
ask you for an RPC endpoint, and write its own script.

9
00:00:29,579 --> 00:00:32,233
Then it hits a revert, error 27,

10
00:00:32,273 --> 00:00:37,876
because Aave actually takes wstETH, not stETH.

11
00:00:37,916 --> 00:00:41,405
And then it asks for your private key in a .env file.

12
00:00:41,445 --> 00:00:44,424
On the right it's the same model, through Aomi.

13
00:00:44,464 --> 00:00:46,851
It reads the wrap rate, simulates the stake,

14
00:00:46,891 --> 00:00:48,116
checks the loan-to-value,

15
00:00:48,156 --> 00:00:52,012
and all six steps pass on a fork as one bundle.

16
00:00:52,562 --> 00:00:55,924
Solana is the same story, just with different failures.

17
00:00:55,964 --> 00:00:59,000
Claude gets rate-limited on the public RPC,

18
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:01,566
forgets the mSOL token account,

19
00:01:01,606 --> 00:01:03,749
misses Kamino's refresh instructions,

20
00:01:03,789 --> 00:01:06,314
and then hits the transaction size limit.

21
00:01:06,354 --> 00:01:08,659
Aomi already knows those accounts and rules,

22
00:01:08,699 --> 00:01:11,039
so it simulates everything in LiteSVM and

23
00:01:11,079 --> 00:01:13,856
packs it into one versioned transaction.

24
00:01:14,107 --> 00:01:15,449
Same model, same request.

25
00:01:15,489 --> 00:01:17,178
The difference is basically how much

26
00:01:17,218 --> 00:01:19,612
work the model has to do by itself.

27
00:01:19,862 --> 00:01:21,726
That's what an execution harness is.

28
00:01:21,766 --> 00:01:24,710
It's the loop around the model: it gets the context,

29
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:26,416
builds the call, simulates it,

30
00:01:26,456 --> 00:01:28,634
and only then commits to your wallet.

31
00:01:28,674 --> 00:01:30,805
And if something fails, it goes back,

32
00:01:30,845 --> 00:01:33,262
reads again, and fixes the call.

33
00:01:33,712 --> 00:01:36,087
First, context instantiation.

34
00:01:36,127 --> 00:01:39,222
Reading chain state and wallet state is deterministic,

35
00:01:39,262 --> 00:01:40,940
so it doesn't need intelligence.

36
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:43,935
It can basically be shrunk to a software function.

37
00:01:43,975 --> 00:01:46,640
Aomi runs its own nodes and keeps a database

38
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:49,404
of verified contracts and Solana programs,

39
00:01:49,444 --> 00:01:50,994
so what took Claude a dozen

40
00:01:51,034 --> 00:01:53,845
calls is one call for the model.

41
00:01:54,095 --> 00:01:55,959
Then transaction construction.

42
00:01:55,999 --> 00:01:57,770
The model just says what to call.

43
00:01:57,810 --> 00:01:59,036
The harness builds the bytes,

44
00:01:59,076 --> 00:02:01,636
and its protocol skills already know the rules,

45
00:02:01,676 --> 00:02:04,028
like Aave wanting wstETH,

46
00:02:04,068 --> 00:02:06,687
or Kamino needing a reserve refresh.

47
00:02:06,727 --> 00:02:09,352
Those are the things Claude had to learn by failing.

48
00:02:12,802 --> 00:02:14,782
Those same failures still happen here,

49
00:02:14,822 --> 00:02:18,493
but they happen on a fork, before anything is sent.

50
00:02:20,143 --> 00:02:23,342
Every call runs against a fork of live state first.

51
00:02:23,382 --> 00:02:24,190
When it reverts,

52
00:02:24,230 --> 00:02:27,034
the error comes back to the model as back pressure,

53
00:02:27,074 --> 00:02:29,646
and only a green run gets committed.

54
00:02:29,686 --> 00:02:32,804
It's the same idea as CI for coding agents:

55
00:02:32,844 --> 00:02:34,952
tests push back when something breaks,

56
00:02:34,992 --> 00:02:37,350
so the model writes good code.

57
00:02:42,001 --> 00:02:43,250
Once everything passes,

58
00:02:43,290 --> 00:02:47,000
Aomi compresses the whole sequence into one transaction.

59
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:48,881
On Ethereum that's a smart wallet

60
00:02:48,921 --> 00:02:50,750
bundle with account abstraction,

61
00:02:50,790 --> 00:02:52,642
and on Solana it's one versioned

62
00:02:52,682 --> 00:02:54,860
transaction with a lookup table.

63
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:58,261
The harness does this to save gas and settlement time.

64
00:02:58,301 --> 00:03:02,227
Compressing deterministic work means the model does less.

65
00:03:02,477 --> 00:03:04,701
And the key never goes to the model.

66
00:03:04,741 --> 00:03:07,580
Commit hands your wallet a signable transaction,

67
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:08,428
and you sign it,

68
00:03:08,468 --> 00:03:11,144
or a scoped session or an agent wallet

69
00:03:11,184 --> 00:03:13,176
signs within the limits you set.

70
00:03:13,216 --> 00:03:17,642
On Solana, a Swig role enforces those limits onchain.

71
00:03:19,892 --> 00:03:23,679
Then the chain settles it, like any other transaction.

72
00:03:26,329 --> 00:03:28,983
After that, Aomi checks the onchain result

73
00:03:29,023 --> 00:03:30,910
against the simulation you approved,

74
00:03:30,950 --> 00:03:33,917
and keeps one record from instruction to receipt.

75
00:03:33,957 --> 00:03:36,448
Claude's version ends with check Etherscan.

76
00:03:36,488 --> 00:03:39,914
This is the part a fund actually needs to audit.

77
00:03:40,365 --> 00:03:42,340
So, same model, same request.

78
00:03:42,790 --> 00:03:44,596
The model interprets the intent,

79
00:03:44,636 --> 00:03:45,954
your wallet holds the key,

80
00:03:45,994 --> 00:03:49,270
and Aomi facilitates everything in between.
